<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <style name="Theme.Dicta" parent="android:Theme.Material.NoActionBar">
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@color/dicta_bg</item>
        <item name="android:colorAccent">@color/dicta_accent</item>
        <item name="android:textColorPrimary">@color/dicta_text</item>
    </style>

    <!--
      Floating overlay: a centred card over whatever is underneath, which stays
      visible and dimmed.

      Measured facts behind each line (see DictateOverlayThemeTest):

      * windowIsFloating is already inherited as true from
        Theme.Material.Dialog — it resolves true even with the old theme, so
        "the window is not floating" was NOT the fullscreen cause. It is pinned
        explicitly so a future parent change cannot silently turn the activity
        back into a fullscreen window.

      * windowBackground must be a drawable that reports padding. A dialog's
        screen margin on Android is decor padding taken from this drawable
        (Drawable.getPadding -> View padding in setBackgroundDrawable), NOT a
        layout margin. The previous value — @android:color/transparent — is a
        ColorDrawable, which reports no padding, so the decor inset became zero
        and the card sat flush against the screen edges.
        @drawable/overlay_window_bg is an <inset> that restores it, by the same
        mechanism @android:drawable/dialog_background_material uses.

      * The width cap is NOT here. windowFixedWidthMinor/Major — the only theme
        attributes that can make a floating window narrower than its content
        wants — are private platform attributes and fail to link from an app
        theme ("style attribute 'android:attr/windowFixedWidthMinor' is
        private"). windowMinWidth* can only widen. So the cap is applied in code
        via Window.setLayout(), from @fraction/overlay_width_fraction. See
        DictateActivity.applyFloatingWindowSize().

      * windowAnimationStyle is deliberately NOT overridden: it already resolves
        to Animation.Material.Dialog through the parent (measured: 0x10302ff),
        i.e. a dialog fade rather than an activity-launch slide. The public
        alternatives are all downgrades, and @android:style/Animation.Material.Dialog
        is itself a private resource.
    -->
    <style name="Theme.Dicta.Overlay" parent="android:Theme.Material.Dialog.NoActionBar">
        <item name="android:windowIsFloating">true</item>
        <item name="android:windowIsTranslucent">true</item>
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@drawable/overlay_window_bg</item>
        <item name="android:windowContentOverlay">@null</item>
        <item name="android:backgroundDimEnabled">true</item>
        <item name="android:backgroundDimAmount">0.6</item>
        <item name="android:windowCloseOnTouchOutside">true</item>
        <item name="android:colorAccent">@color/dicta_accent</item>
        <item name="android:textColorPrimary">@color/dicta_text</item>
    </style>
</resources>
